  <!-- Gazebo hooks for the SPH replay; see src/BODY2/launch/swimming_pool.launch.

       BODY2's URDF is a 24-joint tree standing in for eight real DOF, because
       URDF cannot express the two loop-closure pins per leg (see
       src/BODY2/scripts/prepare_urdf.py).  Replay therefore prescribes every
       tree joint from Body2CoordinateMap rather than the eight hips: an
       under-constrained tree left to a position controller simply comes apart.
       The replay plugin sets all 24 directly and no controller is spawned
       for them.  gazebo_ros_control is loaded all the same, for the joint
       brake described below; its own POSITION write is neutralised by the
       plugin ordering noted at the <gazebo> blocks at the end of this file.

       That is also why a leg can visibly seam, which amph never does: each leg
       is two independent branches off base_link (1.1, 1.2, 1.3 and 2.1, 2.2,
       2.3), and the two pins joining them (P6, Link_2.1 to Link_1.3, and P8,
       Link_1.3 to Link_2.3) are not in the URDF at all; see
       scripts/leg_linkage_sim.py.  The leg is assembled only while all 24
       angles match the coordinate map, and nothing constrains it to.  ODE also
       leaves dParamFMax at 0 unless something sets it, so every hinge is free;
       at 9e-10 kg m2 of principal inertia the torque the SPH coupling feeds
       back through Link::AddTorque drifts a joint by 5 deg, a 5 mm gap at the
       pin, inside a few 1 ms steps.  A dry replay never shows it: nothing
       applies a torque at all.

       Do not read a seam as the validation being wrong without measuring it.
       Both cut pins sit on Link_1.3, which is not between base_link and the
       paddle: Link_2.3 reaches the base through 2.1 -> 2.2 -> 2.3, a plain
       serial chain of prescribed joints, so no gap at P6/P8 can displace it.
       What a *large* gap does mean is that the prescribed angles have left the
       linkage manifold, and then the paddle is misplaced too.

       Closing the loops properly was tried and is worse, so do not repeat it:
       eight SDF <joint> loop closures driven through a
       velocity_controllers/JointTrajectoryController on the eight hips left
       the passive joints at 40 deg RMSE against the kinematic solution, which
       misplaces the paddles far more than any seam does.  A planar mechanism
       built from 3D pins is redundantly constrained: 6 surplus constraints
       per leg, 24 across the robot, hanging off sub-gram links with a 521:1
       mass spread against the hull, which is the case an iterative LCP handles
       worst.  Ball joints (redundancy 6 -> 2 per leg) measured worse still at
       54 deg, and the direct solver, type "world", goes singular and the robot
       comes apart.

       The <limit effort> on every joint above holds the angles on the map.
       gazebo_ros_control reads it and calls SetParam("fmax", effort_limit),
       which is dParamFMax with dParamVel 0, a Coulomb brake, and exactly
       what amph gets.  This file used to set the same bound directly as
       <dynamics friction>; the transmissions replace that route, and the two
       must not both be present.

       3.5 N m is amph's number, a bound rather than a measurement.  Sizing
       it is a threshold, not a gradient: the fluid torque about a hinge peaks
       near 0.24 N m, and below that the joint runs away while above it the
       joint does not move at all.  Real pin friction here would be ~1e-4 N m,
       but nothing is trying to model that; it says "prescribed, so rigid".
       It costs nothing while idle, because ODE only ever applies the impulse
       actually needed and clips at the bound.  Being a constraint rather than
       a force it is stable at any inertia, unlike <dynamics damping>, which
       Gazebo integrates explicitly unless <implicit_spring_damper> is set and
       would need b < 2I/dt of about 2e-6 here.  Joint::SetPosition writes link
       poses directly, outside the solver, so the brake never fights the
       prescription.

       The <limit> elements are not decoration.  Without them urdfdom leaves
       urdf_joint->limits null, DefaultRobotHWSim::registerJointLimits keeps
       its own default of DBL_MAX, and SetParam("fmax", DBL_MAX) hands ODE an
       infinite bound.  A continuous joint needs no lower/upper, but it does
       need effort and velocity for the brake to have a number at all.

       Do not put pid_gains for these joints on the parameter server either:
       DefaultRobotHWSim switches to POSITION_PID when it finds them and then
       never sets fmax at all.

       The publisher below exists only to feed /BODY2/joint_states to
       stage2_sim_validation/gazebo_replay/validate_sim.py.

       jointName is parsed by splitting on "," after erasing spaces, so it has
       to stay on one line: a newline would end up inside a joint name. -->
  <gazebo>
    <plugin name="joint_state_publisher" filename="libgazebo_ros_joint_state_publisher.so">
      <robotNamespace>/BODY2</robotNamespace>
      <updateRate>100</updateRate>
      <jointName>Joint_FL1_1, Joint_FL1_2, Joint_FL1_3, Joint_FL2_1, Joint_FL2_2, Joint_FL2_3, Joint_FR1_1, Joint_FR1_2, Joint_FR1_3, Joint_FR2_1, Joint_FR2_2, Joint_FR2_3, Joint_BL1_1, Joint_BL1_2, Joint_BL1_3, Joint_BL2_1, Joint_BL2_2, Joint_BL2_3, Joint_BR1_1, Joint_BR1_2, Joint_BR1_3, Joint_BR2_1, Joint_BR2_2, Joint_BR2_3</jointName>
    </plugin>
  </gazebo>

  <transmission name="Joint_BR1_1_trans">
    <type>transmission_interface/SimpleTransmission</type>
    <joint name="Joint_BR1_1">
      <hardwareInterface>hardware_interface/PositionJointInterface</hardwareInterface>
    </joint>
    <actuator name="Joint_BR1_1_motor">
      <hardwareInterface>hardware_interface/PositionJointInterface</hardwareInterface>
      <mechanicalReduction>1</mechanicalReduction>
    </actuator>
  </transmission>

